#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:10
msgid ""
"The complete solution for [node.js](http://nodejs.org) command-line "
"interfaces, inspired by Ruby's [commander](https://github.com/commander-"
"rb/commander).     [API "
"documentation](http://tj.github.com/commander.js/)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:14
msgid "Installation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:18
msgid "Option parsing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:20
msgid ""
"Options with commander are defined with the `.option()` method, also "
"serving as documentation for the options. The example below parses args "
"and options from `process.argv`, leaving remaining args as the "
"`program.args` array which were not consumed by options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:46
msgid ""
"Short flags may be passed as a single arg, for example `-abc` is "
"equivalent to `-a -b -c`. Multi-word options such as \"--template-"
"engine\" are camel-cased, becoming `program.templateEngine` etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:48
msgid ""
"Note that multi-word options starting with `--no` prefix negate the "
"boolean value of the following word. For example, `--no-sauce` sets the "
"value of `program.sauce` to false."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:68
msgid ""
"To get string arguments from options you will need to use angle brackets "
"<> for required inputs or square brackets [] for optional inputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:70
msgid "e.g. ```.option('-m --myarg [myVar]', 'my super cool description')```"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:72
msgid "Then to access the input if it was passed in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:74
msgid "e.g. ```var myInput = program.myarg```"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:76
msgid ""
"**NOTE**: If you pass a argument without using brackets the example above"
" will return true and not the value passed in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:79
msgid "Version option"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:81
msgid ""
"Calling the `version` implicitly adds the `-V` and `--version` options to"
" the command. When either of these options is present, the command prints"
" the version number and exits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:87
msgid ""
"If you want your program to respond to the `-v` option instead of the "
"`-V` option, simply pass custom flags to the `version` method using the "
"same syntax as the `option` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:94
msgid "The version flags can be named anything, but the long option is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:96
msgid "Command-specific options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:98
msgid "You can attach options to a command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:115
msgid ""
"A command's options are validated when the command is used. Any unknown "
"options will be reported as an error. However, if an action-based command"
" does not define an action, then the options are not validated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:117
msgid "Coercion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:160
msgid "Regular Expression"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:172
msgid "Variadic arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:174
msgid ""
"The last argument of a command can be variadic, and only the last "
"argument.  To make an argument variadic you have to  append `...` to the "
"argument name.  Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:201
msgid ""
"An `Array` is used for the value of a variadic argument.  This applies to"
" `program.args` as well as the argument passed  to your action as "
"demonstrated above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:204
msgid "Specify the argument syntax"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:228
msgid ""
"Angled brackets (e.g. `<cmd>`) indicate required input. Square brackets "
"(e.g. `[env]`) indicate optional input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:230
msgid "Git-style sub-commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:244
msgid ""
"When `.command()` is invoked with a description argument, no "
"`.action(callback)` should be called to handle sub-commands, otherwise "
"there will be an error. This tells commander that you're going to use "
"separate executables for sub-commands, much like `git(1)` and other "
"popular tools.   The commander will try to search the executables in the "
"directory of the entry script (like `./examples/pm`) with the name "
"`program-command`, like `pm-install`, `pm-search`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:247
msgid ""
"Options can be passed with the call to `.command()`. Specifying `true` "
"for `opts.noHelp` will remove the subcommand from the generated help "
"output. Specifying `true` for `opts.isDefault` will run the subcommand if"
" no other subcommand is specified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:249
msgid ""
"If the program is designed to be installed globally, make sure the "
"executables have proper modes, like `755`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:251
msgid "`--harmony`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:253
msgid "You can enable `--harmony` option in two ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:254
msgid ""
"Use `#! /usr/bin/env node --harmony` in the sub-commands scripts. Note "
"some os version don’t support this pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:255
msgid ""
"Use the `--harmony` option when call the command, like `node --harmony "
"examples/pm publish`. The `--harmony` option will be preserved when "
"spawning sub-command process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:257
msgid "Automated --help"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:259
msgid ""
"The help information is auto-generated based on the information commander"
" already knows about your program, so the following `--help` info is for "
"free:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:277
msgid "Custom help"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:279
msgid ""
"You can display arbitrary `-h, --help` information  by listening for \"--"
"help\". Commander will automatically  exit once you are done so that the "
"remainder of your program  does not execute causing undesired behaviors, "
"for example  in the following executable \"stuff\" will not output when  "
"`--help` is used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:316
msgid ""
"Yields the following help output when `node script-name.js -h` or `node "
"script-name.js --help` are run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:333
msgid ".outputHelp(cb)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:335
msgid ""
"Output help information without exiting. Optional callback cb allows "
"post-processing of help text before it is displayed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:338
msgid ""
"If you want to display help by default (e.g. if no command was provided),"
" you can use something like:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:358
msgid ".help(cb)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:360
msgid ""
"Output help information and exit immediately.   Optional callback cb "
"allows post-processing of help text before it is displayed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:364
msgid "Custom event listeners"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/commander_Readme.md:365
msgid "You can execute custom actions by listening to command and option events."
msgstr ""

